## 4️⃣ Put it all together – a minimal app

```ts
// index.ts (or any entry point)
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import { registerCacheProtocol } from './registerCacheProtocol';

// 1️⃣ Register the custom protocol
registerCacheProtocol();

// 2️⃣ Create the map – the style can now reference cache:// URLs
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // <div id="map"></div> in your HTML
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // The source points to the custom scheme.
      // The `tiles` array can contain multiple URLs; we only need one.
      myCache: {
        type: 'vector',
        tiles: ['cache://tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}'],
        // The maxzoom should match the source data you have cached.
        maxzoom: 14,
        // Optional: give a minzoom if you only cache a subset.
        minzoom: 0,
      },
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'my-fill',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myCache',
        'source-layer': 'my_layer_name', // <-- whatever layer name is inside the MVT
        paint: {
          'fill-color': '#3bb2d0',
          'fill-opacity': 0.6,
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});
```

### What you see in the network tab

* When a tile is **already cached**, the request never leaves the browser – you’ll see a `cache://…` entry in MapLibre’s internal logs but no HTTP request.
* When a tile is **missing**, the fallback `fetch()` runs (see the console log) and you’ll see a normal `https://…` request. After the first successful fetch the tile is stored in IndexedDB, so subsequent pans/zooms hit the cache.

